      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#0 · Dauer im Wechsel, lines 1–8 (Klett witness; Nims translation uncollated)",
          "title": "The wish for one hour — and the west wind",
          "italic": "The opening demonstrates a wish already answered by loss: spring's passing is felt from inside the pleasure it gives.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The speaker asks for one hour in which the early blessing could be held. Already the mild west wind shakes down a shower of blossom; the force carrying beauty away is gentle, and the loss begins within the weather that belongs to its flourishing. Green foliage offers another pleasure — its shade just enjoyed — but the speaker sees autumn within that present gift: the leaves will turn pale",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "and a storm will scatter them. The first eight lines move through a wish, an accomplished loss, a question about enjoyment and an anticipated loss. The whole follows all forty German lines in the Klett 2010 witness, citing the Frankfurt edition of 1988; the project's alias \"Permanence and Change\" names the same poem, and Nims's page 169 remains uncollated — the account uses the German whole.\n\nOccurrences: the blossom-shower (#0); the west wind (#0); the shade and anticipated storm (#0).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
