      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#1 · lines 9–16",
          "title": "Fruit, valley, river",
          "italic": "The stanza demonstrates different temporal movements held together — arriving, receiving and passing — before the one who notices change becomes its subject.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The second stanza answers the wish to hold with an instruction to take one's share: whoever wants the fruit must reach for it promptly, ripening and germination overlapping — what passes includes both the loss of a present gift and the advent of another. Rain enlarges the movement from plant to landscape: every downpour changes the beloved valley, yet affection gives no exemption from alteration. Then the river: one does not swim in the same river a second time. The swimmer participates bodily in what had been observed from shade and bank; water carries the scene forward through a medium that cannot be kept for a repeat encounter.",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.55,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 620
        },
        {
          "id": "occ",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "Occurrences: the prompt reaching (#1); the rains on the valley (#1); the second swim (#1, echoed by the wave at #3).",
          "x": 0.765,
          "y": 0.55,
          "width": 300,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
