      "text": [
        {
          "id": "title",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "#2–#3 · lines 17–32",
          "title": "The changing eyes; the lip, the foot, the hand",
          "italic": "The poem carries the person who wanted to retain the blossom into the very loss desire first placed before itself — no immovable observer stands outside the changes.",
          "body": "",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.05,
          "width": 560,
          "size": 30,
          "align": "left"
        },
        {
          "id": "body1",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "The poem turns on the addressee: walls and palaces appear rock-solid, but they are continually seen with different eyes — recognition has a history on the observer's side. The stanza then leaves the spectator's eyes for bodily powers remembered through their acts: the lip that once found healing in a kiss has passed away; the foot that tested itself against the cliff with a chamois's",
          "x": 0.04,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 620,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left",
          "w": 520
        },
        {
          "id": "body2",
          "visible": true,
          "kicker": "",
          "title": "",
          "italic": "",
          "body": "boldness; the hand once willing and gentle in doing good. Kindness belongs to the history of this body alongside pleasure and daring. Then the name remains where its bearer has altered: what now calls itself by that name arrived like a wave and hurries toward the element — water entering the description of the named person, a recognisable designation continuing across a succession it cannot arrest.\n\nOccurrences: the changing eyes on the walls (#2); the cliff and the chamois-foot (#2); the doing-good hand (#3); the wave and the element (#3, #4).",
          "x": 0.6,
          "y": 0.47,
          "width": 505,
          "size": 14,
          "align": "left"
        }
      ],
